1
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:14,260
Now that we understand filtering
and address translation,

2
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:17,010
let's go over the utility in

3
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:20,310
Linux that supports
that capability.

4
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:23,050
This is IP tables.

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:30,840
IP tables is part of the
broader net filter framework.

6
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:33,580
Here's the address, if you
want to go look it up.

7
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,800
It's a bunch of user
space utilities

8
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,040
that could configure
the Linux kernels

9
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:43,565
filtering framework shown
down here in the picture.

10
00:00:43,565 --> 00:00:46,280
This is just reminder that

11
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,240
the kernel is the data plane

12
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,420
has this packet
processing capabilities,

13
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:53,780
has some tables and
functionality there.

14
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:57,900
Then user space utilities
which we're going to

15
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,630
be using help program

16
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:03,550
what's happening
inside of the kernel.

17
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:08,095
You might have seen some
examples, but if not,

18
00:01:08,095 --> 00:01:11,590
here are several examples of

19
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:14,230
IP tables rules that

20
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:18,910
perform something that
is genuinely useful.

21
00:01:18,910 --> 00:01:21,210
You can look at this and

22
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:24,430
maybe it makes sense to
you and it's so great.

23
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:26,290
If not, well,

24
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:28,430
what we're going to do
is we're going to break

25
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:34,850
down how these commands

26
00:01:34,850 --> 00:01:37,810
are structured and put it in

27
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:41,940
the context of the functionality
that is performing.

28
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:44,730
The basic concepts.

29
00:01:44,730 --> 00:01:46,650
There's three main
things to know

30
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:50,805
about tables, chains and rules.

31
00:01:50,805 --> 00:01:53,150
We can read directly from

32
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:56,690
the IP tables man page and

33
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:58,825
we can see those three concepts

34
00:01:58,825 --> 00:02:00,490
as part of that description,

35
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:03,490
IP tables is used to
set up, maintain,

36
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:07,490
and inspect the tables of
IP packet filter rules.

37
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:08,850
In the Linux kernel,

38
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:11,290
several different
tables may be defined.

39
00:02:11,290 --> 00:02:14,030
Each table contains
a number of built in

40
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:17,975
chains and may also contain
user defined chains.

41
00:02:17,975 --> 00:02:20,330
Each chain is a list of

42
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:24,220
rules which can match
a set of packets.

43
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:26,820
The commands that you'll see

44
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:28,520
are going to generally follow

45
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:30,280
this structure where
we're going to see

46
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:32,670
IP tables as the command name.

47
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:34,915
Then what we're going to do,

48
00:02:34,915 --> 00:02:38,000
we're adding, removing,
changing, whatever.

49
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,010
It's going to be some form
of how do we specify that.

50
00:02:44,010 --> 00:02:47,880
Then it's going to
have the table,

51
00:02:47,880 --> 00:02:51,750
the chain, and the rule.

52
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:54,700
We're going to work
our way backwards.

53
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:57,210
We're going to
start at the rules.

54
00:02:57,210 --> 00:03:02,040
If you recall, rules
in filtering are

55
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:07,225
really just the match
in action in pairs.

56
00:03:07,225 --> 00:03:11,660
A match on some
criteria and then take

57
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:16,830
some action such as drop
or allow a packet through.

58
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:21,720
Let's go over the
basics of matching.

59
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:26,060
Here is coming
from the man page.

60
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:29,785
We could specify
things like -p,--

61
00:03:29,785 --> 00:03:32,660
protocol and then specify

62
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:36,825
a protocol that we want
to match on, say TCP.

63
00:03:36,825 --> 00:03:41,680
We can match the
source IP address,

64
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:43,440
we can match the destination,

65
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:45,900
We can match specific interface

66
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:49,015
and specific output interface.

67
00:03:49,015 --> 00:03:54,760
Those would be some basic
commands that you can or basic

68
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:57,960
parameters or command line

69
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:02,605
arguments that you can
specify for basic matching.

70
00:04:02,605 --> 00:04:08,150
Then there's what is known as
extensions to the matching.

71
00:04:08,150 --> 00:04:12,570
If you specify a -p,--protocol,

72
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:14,460
then there's

73
00:04:14,460 --> 00:04:18,935
some protocol specific
extensions that get loaded.

74
00:04:18,935 --> 00:04:22,130
For TCP for example.

75
00:04:22,130 --> 00:04:24,470
It's saying, great, we can

76
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:28,675
now also match on the
destination port.

77
00:04:28,675 --> 00:04:30,660
Then you can specify the port,

78
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:34,055
you can specify a
range of ports.

79
00:04:34,055 --> 00:04:37,625
What that looks
like is the -p tcp,

80
00:04:37,625 --> 00:04:43,035
that's the basic, but then
specific ones for it.

81
00:04:43,035 --> 00:04:46,665
Destinations one
another is tcp-flags

82
00:04:46,665 --> 00:04:51,700
and these are the flag fields
within the TCP header.

83
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,720
You can match on those.

84
00:04:55,150 --> 00:05:02,130
Alternatively, we could
say -m for module

85
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:07,280
or --match module and

86
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,935
you would specify what
module you want to load.

87
00:05:09,935 --> 00:05:16,115
This allows you to match or
load some match extensions.

88
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:18,780
Shown here are a
couple of examples.

89
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:21,525
The con limit for
connection limit,

90
00:05:21,525 --> 00:05:25,595
we can say -m connlimit.

91
00:05:25,595 --> 00:05:28,505
For this particular module
we want to get loaded.

92
00:05:28,505 --> 00:05:33,365
Then we want to say if the
connection limit is above

93
00:05:33,365 --> 00:05:41,360
16 or the mask is 24,

94
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:45,425
then we're going to match
on this particular rule.

95
00:05:45,425 --> 00:05:49,340
Or if we're doing connection
tracking contract,

96
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:53,220
if we say the ctstate
connection track

97
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:57,380
and state is in the
established or related state,

98
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:00,390
then it's going to
match this rule.

99
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:03,100
These are the general structure

100
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:04,670
for how do you
write the matches.

101
00:06:04,670 --> 00:06:06,540
You can put together

102
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:10,460
several of these into
one long expression.

103
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:14,085
Then you pair it with actions.

104
00:06:14,085 --> 00:06:17,355
Actions would take the form

105
00:06:17,355 --> 00:06:22,810
of -j or --jump to some target.

106
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:28,795
The default targets
are accept or drop.

107
00:06:28,795 --> 00:06:33,055
There's some extra
ones, -j DROP.

108
00:06:33,055 --> 00:06:35,920
You would have your match and

109
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:39,640
then the action is
-j DROP for that.

110
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:42,805
There's also some
extension ones here,

111
00:06:42,805 --> 00:06:45,655
there's reject,

112
00:06:45,655 --> 00:06:50,380
this would REJECT with a
icmp-port-unreachable.

113
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:54,835
It'll actually send a
response to the sender.

114
00:06:54,835 --> 00:06:56,800
You can do logging.

115
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:59,410
We can say -j LOG

116
00:06:59,410 --> 00:07:03,730
and that will have its
specific parameters.

117
00:07:03,730 --> 00:07:07,930
We'll use this nating
for source and

118
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:09,460
destination nating to do

119
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:11,875
some translation
and masquerading

120
00:07:11,875 --> 00:07:14,630
to also do address translation.

121
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:18,865
Now as a reminder,

122
00:07:18,865 --> 00:07:22,030
we talked about tables,
chains and rules.

123
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:28,865
Those rules would be that
part of the command.

124
00:07:28,865 --> 00:07:33,400
Now let's look at tables and
chains and what those are.

125
00:07:34,010 --> 00:07:38,345
There's four defined tables.

126
00:07:38,345 --> 00:07:42,115
Filter, which is
the default table.

127
00:07:42,115 --> 00:07:45,655
Nat, which is the
table that's consulted

128
00:07:45,655 --> 00:07:47,080
when a packet that creates

129
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:49,285
a new connection is encountered.

130
00:07:49,285 --> 00:07:51,850
Mangle, which is used for

131
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:55,540
specialized packet
alteration and raw,

132
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:57,940
used mainly for
configuration exemptions

133
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:00,010
from connection tracking.

134
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:03,070
We'll focus on
these first three,

135
00:08:03,070 --> 00:08:07,040
but let's see where
they fit in though.

136
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:11,575
First, let's zoom
out to understand

137
00:08:11,575 --> 00:08:16,550
how a packet traverses
the Linux kernel.

138
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:21,085
It comes in the device driver,

139
00:08:21,085 --> 00:08:23,290
it's on the receive function,

140
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:24,790
and then it gets
passed and it goes

141
00:08:24,790 --> 00:08:27,500
through this pipeline
of processing.

142
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:34,195
If we zoom in on the IP
layer part of the handling,

143
00:08:34,195 --> 00:08:38,035
we could see that there's
these different hook points.

144
00:08:38,035 --> 00:08:44,515
This is where these various
tables and chains reside

145
00:08:44,515 --> 00:08:47,290
that consist of where
we're going to do

146
00:08:47,290 --> 00:08:51,655
all these decision making
for looking up the rules.

147
00:08:51,655 --> 00:08:55,495
Keep these different
hook points in mind.

148
00:08:55,495 --> 00:08:58,555
But if we go through,

149
00:08:58,555 --> 00:09:01,120
we get a packet,
that's IP packet.

150
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:03,850
Before we even do any routing to

151
00:09:03,850 --> 00:09:07,585
decide is this a
local one or not,

152
00:09:07,585 --> 00:09:09,865
we're going to do
pre-routing hook.

153
00:09:09,865 --> 00:09:12,130
Then we come to do
this routing decision.

154
00:09:12,130 --> 00:09:15,610
Is it input or should
it be forwarded?

155
00:09:15,610 --> 00:09:19,090
If it's input, it'll
come to the input hook.

156
00:09:19,090 --> 00:09:21,190
Like it's local,

157
00:09:21,190 --> 00:09:24,640
meaning it's an
IP address that's

158
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:27,100
associated with
one of the devices

159
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:30,385
on this node, this host.

160
00:09:30,385 --> 00:09:32,890
If not, we go to the
forwarding hook where

161
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:36,340
it goes through the
forwarding packet processing.

162
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:39,220
Then it goes to the
post routing hook

163
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:41,480
before being send out.

164
00:09:42,750 --> 00:09:46,060
The application
would be above this,

165
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:51,025
so shown up here and would
come down and then say,

166
00:09:51,025 --> 00:09:55,255
now I need to send this
particular packet out.

167
00:09:55,255 --> 00:09:57,010
It'll go to the output hook

168
00:09:57,010 --> 00:09:58,330
and then the post routing hook

169
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:05,020
and keep going.
There's four tables.

170
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:08,380
Keep that in mind. We'll
get a little more into

171
00:10:08,380 --> 00:10:12,475
that and then defining
what a chain is.

172
00:10:12,475 --> 00:10:14,110
Chain is just a set of

173
00:10:14,110 --> 00:10:17,935
rules that are
evaluated sequentially.

174
00:10:17,935 --> 00:10:21,595
Rules can be terminating
or non terminating.

175
00:10:21,595 --> 00:10:26,995
If you recall the -j
or jump to a target.

176
00:10:26,995 --> 00:10:28,795
The target can be

177
00:10:28,795 --> 00:10:33,460
user defined chain other than
the one this rule is in.

178
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:37,165
For example, Rule 3 it's
jumping to Chain 2.

179
00:10:37,165 --> 00:10:39,340
It could be one of
the special built in

180
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:41,170
targets which is to
decide the fate of

181
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:45,655
the packet immediately or
one of those extension ones.

182
00:10:45,655 --> 00:10:55,970
This built in ones would be
like ACCEPT and DROP, sorry.

183
00:10:56,370 --> 00:10:59,065
Coming back to this,

184
00:10:59,065 --> 00:11:00,610
so that's what a chain is.

185
00:11:00,610 --> 00:11:05,785
It's a set of these rules that
we sequentially evaluate,

186
00:11:05,785 --> 00:11:07,855
and if it doesn't
match the first rule,

187
00:11:07,855 --> 00:11:11,215
it goes to the second
rule and third rule

188
00:11:11,215 --> 00:11:15,820
and if it match this and
the jump action is this,

189
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:17,815
then we would start evaluating

190
00:11:17,815 --> 00:11:20,185
each of the rules in that chain.

191
00:11:20,185 --> 00:11:24,325
Ultimately it says
ACCEPT or DROP,

192
00:11:24,325 --> 00:11:27,830
then it stops processing
through the chains.

193
00:11:28,950 --> 00:11:32,710
At each hook point that we

194
00:11:32,710 --> 00:11:36,535
talked about, there's
predefined chains.

195
00:11:36,535 --> 00:11:39,790
We can see in this picture,

196
00:11:39,790 --> 00:11:42,190
which I'll zoom in on,

197
00:11:42,190 --> 00:11:48,745
we can see that as part of
pre-routing right here,

198
00:11:48,745 --> 00:11:51,340
there's the mangle
table, nat table.

199
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:52,660
As part of forwarding,

200
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:58,790
there's the mangle table and
filter table, and so forth.

201
00:12:00,930 --> 00:12:03,310
If you recall, filter, nat,

202
00:12:03,310 --> 00:12:05,365
mangle, those are our tables.

203
00:12:05,365 --> 00:12:07,705
Each of those tables consists of

204
00:12:07,705 --> 00:12:11,020
multiple chains and the chains

205
00:12:11,020 --> 00:12:13,720
are named for their hook point.

206
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:18,100
We can see the chain here is

207
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:23,620
called pre-routing as
part of the nat table,

208
00:12:23,620 --> 00:12:26,170
as part of the mangle table.

209
00:12:26,170 --> 00:12:31,585
The chain is called input as
part of the mangle table.

210
00:12:31,585 --> 00:12:34,150
It is called input as
part of the nat table,

211
00:12:34,150 --> 00:12:35,620
it's called input as part of

212
00:12:35,620 --> 00:12:38,730
the filter table, and so forth.

213
00:12:38,730 --> 00:12:42,710
Each table consists
of multiple chains.

214
00:12:42,710 --> 00:12:48,575
Chains are the
sequence of rules.

215
00:12:48,575 --> 00:12:51,965
Now, hopefully it's
starting to come together.

216
00:12:51,965 --> 00:12:57,245
Now we specify tables and
chains as part of this,

217
00:12:57,245 --> 00:13:00,515
each of the IP tables commands.

218
00:13:00,515 --> 00:13:03,920
We say -t<table> to specify

219
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:09,110
what table we're referring to,

220
00:13:09,110 --> 00:13:12,665
and then -a, then the chain.

221
00:13:12,665 --> 00:13:14,825
A for add.

222
00:13:14,825 --> 00:13:19,025
If we're deleting we would
have a different command,

223
00:13:19,025 --> 00:13:21,665
but you would specify the chain.

224
00:13:21,665 --> 00:13:24,140
Now let's look at a
couple of examples.

225
00:13:24,140 --> 00:13:30,065
We say iptables -t
filter -A FORWARD.

226
00:13:30,065 --> 00:13:34,400
This is saying this rule
that we're going to

227
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:41,000
specify applies to the forward
chain of the filter table.

228
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,715
Now, filter is the default one.

229
00:13:43,715 --> 00:13:46,985
If I don't specify
it, it's assumed.

230
00:13:46,985 --> 00:13:50,570
This is the exact
same set of commands.

231
00:13:50,570 --> 00:13:55,400
The second one is
saying in that table,

232
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:57,065
and within that table,

233
00:13:57,065 --> 00:14:00,335
I'm specifically referring
to the pre-routing chain.

234
00:14:00,335 --> 00:14:05,730
Then I'm going to specify
a rule after that.

235
00:14:08,650 --> 00:14:12,710
Now, let's revisit
these examples.

236
00:14:12,710 --> 00:14:16,595
Here, look at what is the table,

237
00:14:16,595 --> 00:14:18,440
what is the chain, what is

238
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:21,095
the match condition,
what is the action?

239
00:14:21,095 --> 00:14:23,555
Take a second, look at this.

240
00:14:23,555 --> 00:14:26,040
Can you actually pick this out?

241
00:14:27,880 --> 00:14:32,105
For the first one,
what's the table?

242
00:14:32,105 --> 00:14:34,310
Well, it's not specified.

243
00:14:34,310 --> 00:14:37,370
If you recall, it would
be the default one,

244
00:14:37,370 --> 00:14:40,355
is -t filter that's the same,

245
00:14:40,355 --> 00:14:42,635
so it's the filter table.

246
00:14:42,635 --> 00:14:45,290
The chain, it's adding a rule to

247
00:14:45,290 --> 00:14:52,190
the forward chain of
the filter table.

248
00:14:52,190 --> 00:14:56,180
Now, what is it going to
do? What's the match?

249
00:14:56,180 --> 00:15:01,745
It's going to match when
this condition is true.

250
00:15:01,745 --> 00:15:06,200
It's saying I want to
load the module state.

251
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:10,130
State has a specific parameter

252
00:15:10,130 --> 00:15:13,655
where it can say
--state ESTABLISHED.

253
00:15:13,655 --> 00:15:17,015
When I match some state

254
00:15:17,015 --> 00:15:19,940
that's being tracked
by this module,

255
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:22,505
it's either established
or related,

256
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:26,435
then this match
condition was true.

257
00:15:26,435 --> 00:15:29,570
Then it goes to the action,

258
00:15:29,570 --> 00:15:32,570
and this would go
to the default or

259
00:15:32,570 --> 00:15:36,500
the built-in action of ACCEPT.

